WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.519
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.519 --> 00:00:04.019
Hi.

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:05.190
I'm Mine Cetinkaya-Rundel.

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.740
I'm a professor at Duke
University and a developer

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.090
educator at Posit.

00:00:09.090 --> 00:00:10.950
And in this video
I'd like to talk

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.220
to you a little bit about how
I use Quarto as an academic.

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.260
We're going to
focus on two parts

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:18.480
of my life as an
academic-- how I

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.610
use Quarto as part
of my teaching,

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:23.290
and how I use Quarto
as part of my research.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.170
So let's start
with the teaching.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.440
We're going to turn
to RStudio and take

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.930
a look at this
document, a simple HTML

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:35.070
document with some code
chunks where we create

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:37.270
a figure and then a table.

00:00:37.270 --> 00:00:40.320
So let's go ahead and
render this document.

00:00:40.320 --> 00:00:42.060
To take a quick
look at the output,

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:44.640
we're doing a very
simple data analysis

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:47.640
with the lovely
palmerpenguins package here.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:49.800
And we'll be able to see
that there are already

00:00:49.800 --> 00:00:51.690
some things built
into the document

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:57.180
I'm using: some hyperlinks
and whatnot in my document.

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.920
I have some cross references
in my document as well.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.190
And what we're going
to do next is enhance

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:07.650
this document for teaching use.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.900
Now, let's suppose
that you are teaching

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:11.620
coding to your students.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.800
One of the things that's
often helpful to do

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.730
is to encourage them to read
the documentation for functions

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.020
whenever possible.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:20.880
And generally we
tell our students,

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:22.500
why don't you
Google the function?

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:23.890
I think we can do one better.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:27.060
So one of the nice
functionalities of Quarto

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:30.270
is that we can actually
add automatic hyperlinks

00:01:30.270 --> 00:01:31.960
to each of our functions.

00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:34.320
So what I'm doing
is in the YAML I

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:39.540
am adding a new field called
"code link," and let's go ahead

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.070
and render that.

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:42.070
And here we go.

00:01:42.070 --> 00:01:45.420
We can see that each of our R
functions are now hyperlinks.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.750
So if I was to click on
a function like library,

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:54.780
I get directed to the
HTML documentation

00:01:54.780 --> 00:01:55.802
for that function.

00:01:55.802 --> 00:01:57.510
And let's go ahead
and click on something

00:01:57.510 --> 00:01:58.860
that is from the tidyverse.

00:01:58.860 --> 00:02:01.740
For example, what
does geom_point do?

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:05.970
So for one of these packages
that has a pkgdown site,

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.910
the code link takes me
directly to the documentation

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.080
for that function.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.240
And particularly
for ggplot functions

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:14.970
these are extremely helpful,
because then your students

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:17.160
can scroll down to
the examples and not

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:20.130
only see the examples as
part of the documentation,

00:02:20.130 --> 00:02:24.580
but the rendered versions
of those plots as well.

00:02:24.580 --> 00:02:27.510
Next let's go and
do something else.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:30.990
We are going to add
a code chunk option.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:36.090
And I am going to say I want
to hide this code chunk,

00:02:36.090 --> 00:02:39.850
and I want to hide any of its
warnings and messages as well.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:45.090
So in order to do that, I
will say, include false,

00:02:45.090 --> 00:02:48.150
and let's go ahead
and render this.

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:50.520
We can see that it
doesn't render because I

00:02:50.520 --> 00:02:52.110
have made a syntax error.

00:02:52.110 --> 00:02:54.720
But the nice thing in
the context of teaching,

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.330
particularly your
student's learning Quarto,

00:02:57.330 --> 00:03:00.030
and particularly if they're
coming at it from having

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.550
learned R Markdown
previously, is

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.630
that when you make a
syntax error like this,

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:12.040
the message that you get
from Quarto is really useful,

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:15.780
and it tells you exactly
where the issue is.

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:18.990
And so let me go
ahead and fix that.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:21.180
I should be using a
semicolon instead.

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:23.800
And once I do that,
if I click on the tab,

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.460
I can actually use YAML
completion to make sure

00:03:26.460 --> 00:03:30.600
that I am typing "false"
in exactly the way

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:32.430
Quarto expects me to.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:34.770
And we can see here
now that that code

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:39.300
chunk where we're loading
packages is not in our output

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:40.570
anymore.

00:03:40.570 --> 00:03:43.710
So as a professor, one of
the things I do regularly

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.950
is go from writing
course notes or a book

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.910
at times to writing slides
for in-class teaching.

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:54.150
And it's really useful when
I can use the same source

00:03:54.150 --> 00:03:58.410
document as a starting point for
each of these different venues.

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:01.350
And with Quarto, it's
really neat that you can

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:04.860
without having to change any
of the text in your document,

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:07.120
simply by changing your format.

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.825
So in this case, we
will make this reveal.js

00:04:09.825 --> 00:04:13.350
to create HTML-based slides.

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.529
It's going to ask us to
reload our visual editor.

00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:19.510
And let's go ahead and render
and see what that looks like.

00:04:19.510 --> 00:04:20.010
All right.

00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:22.079
And now we have a
slide deck version

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:25.240
of our document with
a handy sidebar tool.

00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:28.350
And one of the things
that I really enjoy here

00:04:28.350 --> 00:04:32.250
is that I can not only
make my slides full screen

00:04:32.250 --> 00:04:34.050
or take a look at
a speaker view,

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:36.900
but a question I get asked
very often from my students

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.840
is, can we get a PDF
version of the slides

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:42.870
so that they can annotate
on it, say, on their tablets

00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:43.590
or something.

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:47.130
And there is a neat PDF export
mode that they can click on

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.520
and they can immediately
get the PDF export

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:54.910
should they want to print
it or save it as a PDF file.

00:04:54.910 --> 00:04:57.720
Next, let's take a
look at how might I

00:04:57.720 --> 00:05:01.760
use Quarto in the context of
teaching in the classroom.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.940
Oftentimes I am
teaching in a classroom

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:08.350
where there's a projector screen
behind me that happens to cover

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:10.630
the whiteboard, for example.

00:05:10.630 --> 00:05:13.960
Or a student asks a question
and what I really want to do

00:05:13.960 --> 00:05:15.250
is draw on my slides.

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.130
Maybe something like, if I
scroll to the figure here,

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:20.620
maybe something where I
want to annotate my slides

00:05:20.620 --> 00:05:23.650
and show the three
clusters of penguins.

00:05:23.650 --> 00:05:28.610
I can do this by adding
fields to my YAML again.

00:05:28.610 --> 00:05:36.640
So under reveal.js let's
do "chalkboard true"

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.930
and render this again.

00:05:38.930 --> 00:05:42.130
And you can see that in
addition to the little hamburger

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:45.310
menu for the tools
on the sidebar,

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:47.780
I have gained two
new tools here.

00:05:47.780 --> 00:05:50.093
One of them is an
annotation tool.

00:05:50.093 --> 00:05:51.760
I'm going to go ahead
and click on that,

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:53.980
click a color that
I might want to use,

00:05:53.980 --> 00:05:57.130
and I can then use that
to annotate my slides.

00:05:57.130 --> 00:05:59.080
And the neat thing
is you can actually

00:05:59.080 --> 00:06:02.270
save an annotated version
of your slides as well.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:06.190
So if you tend to annotate
on your slides a lot,

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:09.430
maybe do some derivations
as you go through the class

00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:11.710
and then would like to
post an annotated version

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:14.230
of your slides, you
could save those.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:16.360
I'm going to go ahead and
remove these annotations

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:19.360
by clicking on the Delete
button on my keyboard.

00:06:19.360 --> 00:06:22.390
And now let's get to that
point where I actually

00:06:22.390 --> 00:06:23.950
want to write
something on the board,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:25.780
maybe not just
annotate my slides,

00:06:25.780 --> 00:06:28.250
but there is no board to
be seen in the classroom.

00:06:28.250 --> 00:06:30.790
So I can click on
the Chalkboard option

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:35.350
and basically start typing,
start writing and annotating

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:36.970
on my chalkboard as well.

00:06:36.970 --> 00:06:40.450
And I'm going to go ahead
and get rid of that too now.

00:06:40.450 --> 00:06:45.040
Another handy tool is one where
as you advance your slides,

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.620
the posted version of
your slides advance

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:48.800
along with you as well.

00:06:48.800 --> 00:06:51.550
This can be particularly
helpful for students maybe

00:06:51.550 --> 00:06:55.390
who may have lost where you
were at in that lecture,

00:06:55.390 --> 00:06:58.330
so that as you advance
through your slides,

00:06:58.330 --> 00:07:00.220
if they have it open
on their computers,

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:01.540
they can follow along.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:04.810
In order to do that, I'm going
to add the multiplex option

00:07:04.810 --> 00:07:06.340
to my slides.

00:07:06.340 --> 00:07:09.040
And when we render
this, we can see

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:12.940
that we have now gained two
outputs to our slide deck.

00:07:12.940 --> 00:07:16.180
One of them is
just penguins.html,

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:17.950
which is what we
had before as well,

00:07:17.950 --> 00:07:21.880
and the other one is
penguins-speaker.html.

00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:25.870
So what you want to do is you
want to be posting the .HTML

00:07:25.870 --> 00:07:28.360
version on, say,
your course website,

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:33.070
but you want to be presenting
from your "-speaker" version,

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:35.350
and that way, as you
advance your slides,

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:37.970
they will advance for
your audience as well.

00:07:37.970 --> 00:07:42.470
By default, when we
look at our HTML slides,

00:07:42.470 --> 00:07:45.760
you'll see that the code
is not visible here.

00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:48.910
The reveal.js mode by
default hides your code,

00:07:48.910 --> 00:07:50.800
which is not helpful
if you're actually

00:07:50.800 --> 00:07:52.250
trying to teach the code.

00:07:52.250 --> 00:07:54.580
So let's go ahead and
turn all of that code

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:59.860
on using the execute option
in our document YAML.

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:01.870
You can see that
I can control here

00:08:01.870 --> 00:08:04.790
a bunch of things about
how the code is executed,

00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:07.810
so I'm going to go ahead
and set that to true,

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:11.110
and let's go ahead and render
the document one more time.

00:08:11.110 --> 00:08:13.660
I can see my code,
and underneath it

00:08:13.660 --> 00:08:15.400
I can see the table output.

00:08:15.400 --> 00:08:18.190
If we go back to
the previous slide

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:21.970
where I have some lengthy
ggplot code, and suppose that

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:24.310
I want to give some
insight into this code.

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:27.940
Suppose that I want to talk
a little bit about what

00:08:27.940 --> 00:08:31.330
the scale_color_colorblind
function does and then maybe

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.780
a little bit about what
these theme functions do.

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:37.299
So I might want to highlight
my code, so let's go ahead

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:38.799
and see how we can do that.

00:08:38.799 --> 00:08:43.380
In the relevant
code chunk I'm going

00:08:43.380 --> 00:08:48.220
to add a code chunk option
that says, "code line numbers,"

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:52.930
and in here I am going to say
that a vertical bar to indicate

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:53.740
a pause.

00:08:53.740 --> 00:08:56.920
I want to first talk
about line number nine.

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:59.170
That's the
scale_color_colorblind.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:06.670
And then I want to talk
about lines 10 to 11.

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:08.880
And let's render this again.

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:11.400
If I now advance
my slides, I'll see

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.920
that first, line number
nine is highlighted,

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:15.870
so I can talk a
little bit about that.

00:09:15.870 --> 00:09:18.000
And then I advance
again, and I can

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:21.130
see that now lines 10
and 11 are highlighted,

00:09:21.130 --> 00:09:23.100
and I can render them again.

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:25.320
The neat thing about
this is that we

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:28.830
are able to specify this
as one of our chunk options

00:09:28.830 --> 00:09:31.800
as opposed to having
to edit our code

00:09:31.800 --> 00:09:35.440
and add syntax to the
code chunk itself.

00:09:35.440 --> 00:09:37.380
Now, the problem
here, though, is

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.950
that my plot is really
squished because reveal.js

00:09:40.950 --> 00:09:43.740
is giving the space
for the code chunk

00:09:43.740 --> 00:09:47.310
itself and then using
whatever space is available

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:50.550
that's left over in this slide
for that plot, which is not

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:52.110
very helpful.

00:09:52.110 --> 00:09:54.060
One approach to
solving this would

00:09:54.060 --> 00:09:58.740
be to create another code chunk
that references this one using

00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:02.340
its label, but a neat
Quarto solution to this

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:06.780
is to use the code chunk
option Output Location.

00:10:06.780 --> 00:10:09.390
And I can choose to--

00:10:09.390 --> 00:10:11.340
the default means
that the output

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:14.490
is going to show up
underneath your code chunk.

00:10:14.490 --> 00:10:18.450
Fragment means it's going
to appear when you advance

00:10:18.450 --> 00:10:21.690
the slides, and the
Slide option says

00:10:21.690 --> 00:10:24.310
to put the output
on the next slide.

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:27.270
So let's select that so that
we can get a nice big plot

00:10:27.270 --> 00:10:29.440
on the next slide.

00:10:29.440 --> 00:10:29.940
All right.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:33.030
So I have just my
code on this slide.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.600
I can advance through and
see the highlighted versions

00:10:36.600 --> 00:10:38.580
of portions of that code.

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:42.600
And now my plot itself has
moved to the next slide.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:44.340
Now suppose that
you are actually

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:46.650
using Quarto to teach Quarto.

00:10:46.650 --> 00:10:48.540
You actually want to
show your students

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.960
how you can add all of
these code chunk options.

00:10:51.960 --> 00:10:53.670
In the rendered
document they are

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:56.970
impossible to see since
they're hidden from the output.

00:10:56.970 --> 00:11:01.200
But we can easily reveal those
by adding a new code chunk

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:05.100
option called "echo fenced."

00:11:05.100 --> 00:11:07.060
And let's go ahead
and render that too.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:12.480
And now our code chunk actually
prints not just the code itself

00:11:12.480 --> 00:11:16.270
but all of the code
chunk options as well.

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:21.270
So I didn't have to create a
new sort of indented code chunk

00:11:21.270 --> 00:11:24.660
or something to be
able to reveal these.

00:11:24.660 --> 00:11:27.540
And you can see that the only
code chunk option that's not

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:29.880
visible here is the echo fence.

00:11:29.880 --> 00:11:32.160
So it's smart enough
to hide that one

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.500
and show the rest of
the code chunk options

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:38.040
so you can easily talk about
how to write Quarto documents

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:40.900
with your learners as well.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:45.930
Now we've added a lot of
options to these slides that

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:48.400
are very much slide specific.

00:11:48.400 --> 00:11:52.330
Let's go back and turn things
back to an HTML document.

00:11:52.330 --> 00:11:55.260
So I'm going to change
my format to HTML,

00:11:55.260 --> 00:11:59.170
which asks me to reload the
visual editor one more time.

00:11:59.170 --> 00:12:00.990
And let's go ahead
and render that again.

00:12:00.990 --> 00:12:02.130
All right.

00:12:02.130 --> 00:12:05.670
And let's talk about one
last code chunk option

00:12:05.670 --> 00:12:08.040
that can be really helpful
in a teaching context,

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.690
particularly if you are writing
long-form documents, course

00:12:12.690 --> 00:12:15.210
notes, or a book
where you might want

00:12:15.210 --> 00:12:17.400
to annotate your code further.

00:12:17.400 --> 00:12:20.910
I'm going to scroll down
to the very last code chunk

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:23.020
here where we're
creating a table,

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:25.290
and I'm going to add
some annotation to it.

00:12:25.290 --> 00:12:29.160
So I might say this
is my line number one.

00:12:29.160 --> 00:12:34.920
So the hash sign and then one.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:37.350
And we're going to
basically annotate

00:12:37.350 --> 00:12:40.070
each one of these line numbers.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:47.940
And let's go ahead
and say this is four.

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:50.370
And right underneath
my code chunk,

00:12:50.370 --> 00:12:53.020
I'm going to start
writing up my annotation.

00:12:53.020 --> 00:12:59.880
So something like, "start
with the penguins data frame,

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:11.190
then slice the top 10 rows,
select some variables,

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:14.610
and make a table with gt.

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:19.680
This code annotation
feature that's

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:22.770
available with
Quarto 1.3 onwards

00:13:22.770 --> 00:13:27.900
allows you to annotate
lines, each line of code,

00:13:27.900 --> 00:13:32.297
and basically add some text
to it so that you can--

00:13:32.297 --> 00:13:34.380
if you were teaching in
person, these are probably

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:36.120
the words you would
say about the code,

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:38.580
but now it's really
easy to capture them

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:40.570
in a written document as well.

00:13:40.570 --> 00:13:43.560
And it doesn't have to
be one line per code.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.950
So I could do something like
give the same annotation

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:51.180
number to that last
line of code and say

00:13:51.180 --> 00:13:53.850
that I want to capture
both of those comments

00:13:53.850 --> 00:13:59.550
in a single item in
the list underneath it.

00:13:59.550 --> 00:14:00.510
And here we go.

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:02.910
That item four has
gone away, and now I

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:06.450
have an item three that
basically covers everything

00:14:06.450 --> 00:14:07.860
that's underneath it.

00:14:07.860 --> 00:14:11.010
So we've showcased a
bunch of Quarto features

00:14:11.010 --> 00:14:13.440
that are helpful for
teaching, but what

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:15.180
about the research
or the publication

00:14:15.180 --> 00:14:16.630
side of an academic slide?

00:14:16.630 --> 00:14:18.750
So let's talk about
that a little bit next.

00:14:18.750 --> 00:14:21.030
The neat thing about
writing papers with Quarto

00:14:21.030 --> 00:14:24.660
is that it comes with
some article templates.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:27.240
So I'm on the Quarto
documentation page here,

00:14:27.240 --> 00:14:31.080
and I am going to scroll
down here a little bit

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.780
and go to the GitHub
repository, where

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:38.760
we can find a bunch of
journal templates here.

00:14:38.760 --> 00:14:42.900
I tend to write papers that
use the American Statistical

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.840
Association journals
template, so we're

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:47.640
going to take a
look at that here.

00:14:47.640 --> 00:14:52.320
If the template that you
tend to use for your papers

00:14:52.320 --> 00:14:54.540
is already listed
here, that's fantastic.

00:14:54.540 --> 00:14:56.260
You can get started right away.

00:14:56.260 --> 00:14:58.170
And if not, I
encourage you to open

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:00.000
an issue in the repository.

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:02.670
Or if you're interested,
contribute the template

00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:05.440
to the repository so that
others can use as well.

00:15:05.440 --> 00:15:09.030
So for the JASA template,
it says to install it.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.930
We're going to use quarto
use template, and then

00:15:12.930 --> 00:15:14.550
quarto journals jasa.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:16.890
So I'm going to copy
that line of code,

00:15:16.890 --> 00:15:20.430
and let's go to that page
here as well, go to my RStudio

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:21.735
and open up my terminal.

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:28.760
And let's go ahead
and paste that there.

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:31.710
It asked me if I trust the
authors of this template.

00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:33.200
Yes.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:37.550
The directory name can
be My Awesome Paper,

00:15:37.550 --> 00:15:40.580
and let's go ahead
and see what we got.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:45.140
In my files pane here I can
see a new directory called

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:49.160
My Awesome Paper, and there
is a Quarto file in there

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:51.770
with a very extensive YAML.

00:15:51.770 --> 00:15:55.490
And if you have written papers
before that, whether either

00:15:55.490 --> 00:15:57.710
with R Markdown, with
the articles package,

00:15:57.710 --> 00:16:00.860
or just using LaTeX,
know that the preamble

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:02.430
tends to be pretty long.

00:16:02.430 --> 00:16:09.350
So I am going to give it a
new title, "My Awesome Paper

00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:13.250
About Data Science,"
and maybe I can just

00:16:13.250 --> 00:16:14.690
add my name quickly here.

00:16:18.290 --> 00:16:21.450
And let's go ahead and
render the document.

00:16:21.450 --> 00:16:22.340
And here we go.

00:16:22.340 --> 00:16:24.380
The output looks
like something I'm

00:16:24.380 --> 00:16:27.980
actually pretty used to
seeing when I write papers

00:16:27.980 --> 00:16:29.750
using other tools as well.

00:16:29.750 --> 00:16:31.370
And the neat thing
with this template

00:16:31.370 --> 00:16:34.880
is that it comes with some pre
filled-in text that tells me

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:41.150
exactly how I might include
figures or tables in my output

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:44.970
and how I can even include
some citations as well.

00:16:44.970 --> 00:16:50.270
So let's switch over
to the visual editor,

00:16:50.270 --> 00:16:52.490
and let's talk a little
bit about citation.

00:16:52.490 --> 00:16:56.150
So I would like to add
a citation to my paper.

00:16:56.150 --> 00:16:58.520
Let's say that I want
to add something right

00:16:58.520 --> 00:17:00.560
at the end of my introduction.

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:03.330
And I can do this
in one of two ways.

00:17:03.330 --> 00:17:08.300
I can either use the
Insert menu and say

00:17:08.300 --> 00:17:15.410
that I want to add a citation,
or I can use my Insert Anything

00:17:15.410 --> 00:17:19.829
tool and say citation.

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:23.450
And you can see that I have
even linked up my Zotero library

00:17:23.450 --> 00:17:24.390
here.

00:17:24.390 --> 00:17:25.670
And so I can click on that.

00:17:25.670 --> 00:17:28.760
I happen to have one of my
papers saved in my Zotero

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:29.690
library.

00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:32.870
So if you use a tool
like Zotero or Crossref

00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.840
or something like that,
you can actually link these

00:17:35.840 --> 00:17:37.850
up to your RStudio,
and as long as you

00:17:37.850 --> 00:17:40.880
have them running on your
computer, on the same computer,

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:43.190
they automatically
link up anyway,

00:17:43.190 --> 00:17:45.570
and I can go ahead
and insert that.

00:17:45.570 --> 00:17:49.010
So let's go ahead and save
that and render this document

00:17:49.010 --> 00:17:50.100
one more time.

00:17:50.100 --> 00:17:50.600
Right.

00:17:50.600 --> 00:17:52.370
So at the end of
my introduction,

00:17:52.370 --> 00:17:53.970
the new citation is there.

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:56.360
I can click on it, and I
can see that it has been

00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:58.440
added to my list of references.

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:02.300
And what RStudio also did
for me at the same time

00:18:02.300 --> 00:18:06.780
is it added that entry
to my BIB file as well.

00:18:06.780 --> 00:18:10.890
So I can see that has already
been inserted into my BIB file.

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:13.010
What if you don't use
a tool like Zotero?

00:18:13.010 --> 00:18:16.940
Or maybe you are looking for
a paper on Google Scholar.

00:18:16.940 --> 00:18:21.860
So how about we look for the
Welcome to the Tidyverse paper.

00:18:21.860 --> 00:18:24.650
And I'm going to go
ahead and open that up,

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:27.215
and we're going to grab
the DOI of this paper.

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:36.070
I have copied that from here.

00:18:36.070 --> 00:18:38.580
Let's go back to
RStudio, and let's say

00:18:38.580 --> 00:18:41.250
that we want to
add that citation,

00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:45.030
suppose here, using the
Insert Anything tool again

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:46.410
and citation.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:48.480
I'm going to say
this time that I want

00:18:48.480 --> 00:18:52.350
to find a paper by its DOI.

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:56.700
So I paste the DOI here, and it
goes out and searches for that,

00:18:56.700 --> 00:19:00.210
and it allows me to insert
that citation either

00:19:00.210 --> 00:19:02.760
as an in-text citation or not.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:04.710
Let's go ahead and hit Insert.

00:19:04.710 --> 00:19:07.560
And once I have done
that, that has also

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:09.330
been added to my BIB file.

00:19:12.540 --> 00:19:14.940
We can see that that's
been added to my BIB file,

00:19:14.940 --> 00:19:18.010
and a reference has been
created for it as well.

00:19:18.010 --> 00:19:20.340
And if I render my
document one last time,

00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:23.670
I'll be able to see that that
citation has been inserted

00:19:23.670 --> 00:19:25.540
to the appropriate
location as well,

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:29.010
and I can find the paper
in the list of references

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:30.550
at the end of my document.

00:19:30.550 --> 00:19:33.930
So we've talked a bit about
using Quarto for teaching

00:19:33.930 --> 00:19:35.730
and a little bit
about using Quarto

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:37.830
as part of your research.

00:19:37.830 --> 00:19:40.170
If you've been
intrigued or if you've

00:19:40.170 --> 00:19:43.440
already been a Quarto user and
have enjoyed these features

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:48.330
that I highlighted here,
there is an abundant number

00:19:48.330 --> 00:19:52.680
of features available in Quarto
that we haven't gotten a chance

00:19:52.680 --> 00:19:54.112
to touch on in this video.

00:19:54.112 --> 00:19:55.570
So if you would
like to learn more,

00:19:55.570 --> 00:19:58.110
I encourage you to
go to Quarto.org.

00:19:58.110 --> 00:20:00.810
If you are just getting
started with Quarto,

00:20:00.810 --> 00:20:03.480
the Get Started menu
will be helpful,

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:05.400
where you can learn
about how to use

00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:09.450
Quarto using your editor
of choice, whichever

00:20:09.450 --> 00:20:10.710
one is your favorite.

00:20:10.710 --> 00:20:12.990
And if you have already
dived into Quarto

00:20:12.990 --> 00:20:18.720
and know the basics, I suggest
going to the guide to dive

00:20:18.720 --> 00:20:20.627
further into any of
the different outputs

00:20:20.627 --> 00:20:22.335
that you might be
interested in creating,

00:20:22.335 --> 00:20:25.470
like a website or a book
or adding interactivity

00:20:25.470 --> 00:20:26.610
to your documents.

00:20:26.610 --> 00:20:28.170
And to stay up to
date with what's

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:32.500
happening in Quarto land, the
blog is a great place to go.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:34.920
Particularly when
there are new features,

00:20:34.920 --> 00:20:36.780
they are generally
documented here.

00:20:36.780 --> 00:20:38.010
Thank you very much.

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:41.360
[MUSIC PLAYING]

